window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2023-12-3", "source": "2023年12月真题·第3套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence “With their valuable skills and experience, elderly people can continue to make significant contributions to society.\" You can make comments, cite examples or use your personal experiences to develop your essay. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "With their valuable skills and experience, elderly people can continue to make significant contributions to\nsociety. Far from being a burden, seniors possess a lifetime of professional knowledge and practical wisdom\nthat younger generations simply have not had time to acquire.\nThis potential shows itself in countless ways. Take retired engineers and doctors as an example: many of them\nreturn to their former posts as consultants, training newcomers and solving problems that puzzle less\nexperienced staff. In my own community, retired teachers volunteer to run free evening classes, while elderly\ncraftsmen pass down traditional skills that would otherwise disappear. Such efforts prove that age brings not\ndecline but a rich resource waiting to be tapped.\nTherefore, we should stop regarding old age merely as a time of rest and instead recognize its true worth. If\nsociety creates more opportunities for the elderly to share their expertise, everyone benefits. Only by valuing\nand drawing on their skills and experience can we build a wiser and more harmonious community."}, "translation": {"prompt": "近年来，中国老龄人口持续增长。中国政府正采取各种措施，推进养老服务体系建设，使老年人晚年生活健康幸福。全国兴建了各类养老服务机构。为了提升养老机构的服务质量，政府颁布了一系列标准，加强对养老机构的监管。许多城市为方便老年人用餐，开设了社区食堂，为他们提供价格实惠的饭菜。行动不便的老年人还能享受上门送餐服务。同时，中国还在积极探索居家和社区养老等其他养老模式，以确保所有老年人老有所养。", "sample": "In recent years, the elderly population in China has been growing continuously. In response, the Chinese government is taking various measures to promote the development of the elderly care service system, so that the elderly can enjoy a healthy and happy life in their later years. Various elderly care institutions have been built across the country. To improve the service quality of these institutions, the government has issued a series of standards, strengthening the supervision of them. To make dining more convenient for the elderly, many cities have opened community canteens, which provide affordable meals for them. In addition, elderly people with limited mobility can also enjoy home meal delivery services. At the same time, China is also actively exploring other elderly care models, such as home-based and community-based care, to ensure that all the elderly are well cared for in their old age."}, "listeningUrl": null, "clozePassage": "Modern U.S. cities were designed to make exercise unnecessary. Cars and elevators once symbolized urban areas as machines for more efficient living. Now it is clear that these improvements provide great benefits but also  __26__  health costs. Recent studies show that urban  __27__  encourages more driving and is associated with heavier weight. This  __28__  suggests that the layout and design of cities can hinder or promote healthier lifestyle choices and it is  __29__  that urban planners bear this in mind.\nUnfortunately, urban planning still centers in large part on solving the problems of the past. Of course cities still need to  __30__  standard public health practices, such as separating toxic facilities from homes and restricting heavy truck traffic through  __31__  residential areas. But it's also important to create healthier cities—and the discussion is already underway. More and more city planners are paying increasing attention to encouraging physical activity by making it easier and safer for people to recreate, walk, bike and take public transportation. Many studies of  __32__  show that people live the longest in environments where physical activity is part of everyday life.\nProviding more walkable spaces, better protected bike lanes and more  __33__  spaces are important steps. But even smaller changes can be effective.\nCities can close off streets on weekends to encourage communities to get out and walk. They also can provide more seating in public places, so that less-fit residents can rest during their journeys. Using public spaces in cities as places where people can exercise promotes  __34__  , rather than allowing physical activity to become restricted to private gyms with often-expensive monthly fees that  __35__  less wealthy people from joining.", "matchIntro": "Can Learning a Foreign Language Prevent Dementia?", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "You may have heard that learning another language is one method for preventing or at least postponing the onset of dementia. Dementia refers to the loss of cognitive abilities, and one of its most common forms is Alzheimer's disease(阿尔茨海默氏病). At this time, the causes of the disease are not well understood, and consequently, there are no proven steps that people can take to prevent it. Nonetheless, some researchers have suggested that learning a foreign language might help delay the onset of dementia."}, {"label": "B", "text": "To explore this possibility more deeply, let's look at some of the common misconceptions about dementia and the aging brain. First of all, dementia is not an inevitable part of the normal aging process. Most older adults do not develop Alzheimer's disease or other forms of dementia. It is also important to remember that dementia is not the same thing as normal forgetfulness. At any age, we might experience difficulty finding the exact word we want or have trouble remembering the name of the person we just met. People with dementia have more serious problems, like feeling confused or getting lost in a familiar place. Think of it this way: If you forget where you parked your car at the mall, that's normal; if you forget how to drive a car, that may be a signal that something more serious is going on."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The idea that dementia can be prevented is based on the comparison of the brain to a muscle. When people talk about the brain, they sometimes say things like \"It is important to exercise your brain\" or \"To stay mentally fit, you have to give your brain a workout.\" Although these are colorful analogies, in reality the brain is not a muscle. Unlike muscles, the brain is always active and works even during periods of rest and sleep. In addition, although some muscle cells have a lifespan of only a few days, brain cells last a lifetime. Not only that, but it has been shown that new brain cells are being created throughout one's lifespan."}, {"label": "D", "text": "While it makes for a colorful analogy, comparing the brain to a muscle is inaccurate and misleading. So, if the brain is not a muscle, can it still be exercised? Once again, researchers don't know for sure. There are now many computer, online, and mobile device applications that claim to be able to \"train your brain,\" and they typically tap into a variety of cognitive abilities. However, research suggests that although this type of training may improve one's abilities at the tasks themselves, they don't seem to improve other abilities. In other words, practicing a letter-detection task will, over time, improve your letter-detection skills, but it will not necessarily enhance your other perceptual abilities."}, {"label": "E", "text": "However, there is some reason to believe that learning languages might be different. The best evidence that foreign language learning confers cognitive benefits comes from research with those who are already bilingual(双语的). Bilingualism most commonly occurs when children are exposed to two languages, either in the home (mom speaks Dutch, dad speaks Spanish) or more formally in early schooling. But bilingualism certainly occurs in adulthood as well."}, {"label": "F", "text": "Bilingualism and multilingualism are actually more common than you might think. In fact, it has been estimated that there are fewer monolingual speakers in the world than bilinguals and multilinguals. Although in many countries most inhabitants share just one language, other countries have several official languages. Switzerland, for example, has four official languages: German, French, Italian, and Romansh. Throughout large parts of Africa, Arabic, Swahili, French, and English are often known and used by individuals who speak a different, native language in their home than they do in the marketplace. So bilingualism and multilingualism are to be found throughout the world. And with regard to cognitive abilities, the research on those who speak more than one language paints an encouraging picture."}, {"label": "G", "text": "For one thing, bilinguals are better at multitasking. One explanation of this superiority is that speakers of two languages are continually inhibiting one of their languages, and this process of inhibition confers general cognitive benefits to other activities. In fact, bilingual individuals outperform their monolingual counterparts on a variety of cognitive tasks, such as following complex instructions, and switching to new instructions. For the sake of completeness, it should be noted that the advantages of being bilingual are not universal across all cognitive domains. Bilingual individuals have been shown to have smaller vocabularies and to take longer in retrieving words from memory when compared to monolinguals. In the long run, however, the cognitive and linguistic advantages of being bilingual far outweigh these two drawbacks."}, {"label": "H", "text": "If the benefits of being bilingual spill over to other aspects of cognition, then we would expect to see a lower incidence of Alzheimer's disease in bilinguals than in monolinguals, or at least a later onset of Alzheimer's for bilinguals. In fact, there is evidence to support this claim. The psychologist Ellen Bialystok and her colleagues obtained the histories of 184 individuals who had made use of a memory clinic in Toronto. For those who showed signs of dementia, the monolinguals in the sample had an average age of 71.4 years at time of onset. The bilinguals, in contrast, received their diagnosis at 75.5 years, on average. In a study of this sort, a difference of four years is highly significant, and could not be explained by other systematic differences between the two groups. For example, the monolinguals reported, on average, a year and a half more schooling than their bilingual counterparts, so the effect was clearly not due to formal education."}, {"label": "I", "text": "A separate study, conducted in India, found strikingly similar results: bilingual patients developed symptoms of dementia 4.5 years later than monolinguals, even after other potential factors, such as gender and occupation, were controlled for. In addition, researchers have reported other positive effects of bilingualism for cognitive abilities in later life, even when the person acquired the language in adulthood. Crucially, Bialystok suggested that the positive benefits of being bilingual were only found in those who used both languages all the time."}, {"label": "J", "text": "But encouraging as these kinds of studies are, they still have not established exactly how or why differences between bilinguals and monolinguals exist. Because these studies looked back at the histories of people who were already bilingual, the results can only say that a difference between the two groups was found, but not why that difference occurred. Further research is needed to determine what caused the differences in age of onset between the two groups."}, {"label": "K", "text": "Other studies of successful aging suggest that being connected to one's community and having plenty of social interaction is also important in delaying or even preventing the onset of dementia. Once again, however, the results are far less clear than the popular media might lead you to believe. Older individuals who lead active social lives are, almost by definition, healthier than their counterparts who rarely leave their homes or interact with others. So we can't really say whether being socially active prevents the onset of dementia, or if people who don't have dementia are more likely to be socially active."}, {"label": "L", "text": "But even if studying a foreign language is not a magical cure-all, there is one thing it will do: It will make you a better speaker of a foreign language. Doing that confers a whole host of advantages we do know about."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Research is meant to benefit society by raising public awareness and creating products and innovations that enhance development. For research to serve its full purpose, the results must leave the confines of research laboratories and academic journals.\nFindings effectively communicated can go a long way to serve the interests of the public. They can help address social injustices or improve treatments offered to patients.\nMany researchers seem to be content with sharing the results of their studies in academic journals or at conferences. But few journals allow everybody to read the findings. Even articles freely available are usually written in academic language incomprehensible to the average reader.\nFor researchers in the tenure-track system, their main goal is winning tenure, which in part can be achieved by getting a number of papers published in prominent journals. Pressures like this mean community-level outreach is not prioritised.\nMany researchers lack the writing skills to describe their results to a general audience. They may also worry about whether the public will understand their findings, or about findings being used to influence controversial policies. These concerns cause some researchers to shy away from communicating their findings outside the academic community.\nPropagating research findings beyond academic publications is particularly crucial for addressing certain social discrepancies. It can help families, communities, healthcare providers, policymakers, government agencies and other stakeholders to understand and respond to crises that plague society.\nThe benefits of sharing findings flow both ways. Engaging with other researchers and the public can lead to unexpected new connections and new ideas that could suggest fruitful new directions for research.\nTo benefit both researchers and the communities, the need to find innovative, accessible ways to share the work cannot be overstated.\nInstitutions and funding organisations should support more researchers to publish in open access journals so that the public doesn't have to pay to read them. Institutions and researchers should invest in partnerships that expand capacity for sharing results more broadly.\nFurthermore, ethics committees should make it mandatory for researchers to share their results with the public. Every research participant should opt in or out of receiving results, as part of the process of giving informed consent.\nThere could be misunderstanding of the findings presented by the researcher because of technical terms. But this can be resolved by researchers engaging the services of professional writers or communication officers to help with translating their study into more accessible language and share it widely with media outlets and the public.\nSharing results with the people who are most affected by them makes us better researchers and ensures that our work can be used to improve people's lives. Institutions and collaborators must recognise the value of doing so.", "47": "Research is meant to benefit society by raising public awareness and creating products and innovations that enhance development. For research to serve its full purpose, the results must leave the confines of research laboratories and academic journals.\nFindings effectively communicated can go a long way to serve the interests of the public. They can help address social injustices or improve treatments offered to patients.\nMany researchers seem to be content with sharing the results of their studies in academic journals or at conferences. But few journals allow everybody to read the findings. Even articles freely available are usually written in academic language incomprehensible to the average reader.\nFor researchers in the tenure-track system, their main goal is winning tenure, which in part can be achieved by getting a number of papers published in prominent journals. Pressures like this mean community-level outreach is not prioritised.\nMany researchers lack the writing skills to describe their results to a general audience. They may also worry about whether the public will understand their findings, or about findings being used to influence controversial policies. These concerns cause some researchers to shy away from communicating their findings outside the academic community.\nPropagating research findings beyond academic publications is particularly crucial for addressing certain social discrepancies. It can help families, communities, healthcare providers, policymakers, government agencies and other stakeholders to understand and respond to crises that plague society.\nThe benefits of sharing findings flow both ways. Engaging with other researchers and the public can lead to unexpected new connections and new ideas that could suggest fruitful new directions for research.\nTo benefit both researchers and the communities, the need to find innovative, accessible ways to share the work cannot be overstated.\nInstitutions and funding organisations should support more researchers to publish in open access journals so that the public doesn't have to pay to read them. Institutions and researchers should invest in partnerships that expand capacity for sharing results more broadly.\nFurthermore, ethics committees should make it mandatory for researchers to share their results with the public. Every research participant should opt in or out of receiving results, as part of the process of giving informed consent.\nThere could be misunderstanding of the findings presented by the researcher because of technical terms. But this can be resolved by researchers engaging the services of professional writers or communication officers to help with translating their study into more accessible language and share it widely with media outlets and the public.\nSharing results with the people who are most affected by them makes us better researchers and ensures that our work can be used to improve people's lives. Institutions and collaborators must recognise the value of doing so.", "48": "Research is meant to benefit society by raising public awareness and creating products and innovations that enhance development. For research to serve its full purpose, the results must leave the confines of research laboratories and academic journals.\nFindings effectively communicated can go a long way to serve the interests of the public. They can help address social injustices or improve treatments offered to patients.\nMany researchers seem to be content with sharing the results of their studies in academic journals or at conferences. But few journals allow everybody to read the findings. Even articles freely available are usually written in academic language incomprehensible to the average reader.\nFor researchers in the tenure-track system, their main goal is winning tenure, which in part can be achieved by getting a number of papers published in prominent journals. Pressures like this mean community-level outreach is not prioritised.\nMany researchers lack the writing skills to describe their results to a general audience. They may also worry about whether the public will understand their findings, or about findings being used to influence controversial policies. These concerns cause some researchers to shy away from communicating their findings outside the academic community.\nPropagating research findings beyond academic publications is particularly crucial for addressing certain social discrepancies. It can help families, communities, healthcare providers, policymakers, government agencies and other stakeholders to understand and respond to crises that plague society.\nThe benefits of sharing findings flow both ways. Engaging with other researchers and the public can lead to unexpected new connections and new ideas that could suggest fruitful new directions for research.\nTo benefit both researchers and the communities, the need to find innovative, accessible ways to share the work cannot be overstated.\nInstitutions and funding organisations should support more researchers to publish in open access journals so that the public doesn't have to pay to read them. Institutions and researchers should invest in partnerships that expand capacity for sharing results more broadly.\nFurthermore, ethics committees should make it mandatory for researchers to share their results with the public. Every research participant should opt in or out of receiving results, as part of the process of giving informed consent.\nThere could be misunderstanding of the findings presented by the researcher because of technical terms. But this can be resolved by researchers engaging the services of professional writers or communication officers to help with translating their study into more accessible language and share it widely with media outlets and the public.\nSharing results with the people who are most affected by them makes us better researchers and ensures that our work can be used to improve people's lives. Institutions and collaborators must recognise the value of doing so.", "49": "Research is meant to benefit society by raising public awareness and creating products and innovations that enhance development. For research to serve its full purpose, the results must leave the confines of research laboratories and academic journals.\nFindings effectively communicated can go a long way to serve the interests of the public. They can help address social injustices or improve treatments offered to patients.\nMany researchers seem to be content with sharing the results of their studies in academic journals or at conferences. But few journals allow everybody to read the findings. Even articles freely available are usually written in academic language incomprehensible to the average reader.\nFor researchers in the tenure-track system, their main goal is winning tenure, which in part can be achieved by getting a number of papers published in prominent journals. Pressures like this mean community-level outreach is not prioritised.\nMany researchers lack the writing skills to describe their results to a general audience. They may also worry about whether the public will understand their findings, or about findings being used to influence controversial policies. These concerns cause some researchers to shy away from communicating their findings outside the academic community.\nPropagating research findings beyond academic publications is particularly crucial for addressing certain social discrepancies. It can help families, communities, healthcare providers, policymakers, government agencies and other stakeholders to understand and respond to crises that plague society.\nThe benefits of sharing findings flow both ways. Engaging with other researchers and the public can lead to unexpected new connections and new ideas that could suggest fruitful new directions for research.\nTo benefit both researchers and the communities, the need to find innovative, accessible ways to share the work cannot be overstated.\nInstitutions and funding organisations should support more researchers to publish in open access journals so that the public doesn't have to pay to read them. Institutions and researchers should invest in partnerships that expand capacity for sharing results more broadly.\nFurthermore, ethics committees should make it mandatory for researchers to share their results with the public. Every research participant should opt in or out of receiving results, as part of the process of giving informed consent.\nThere could be misunderstanding of the findings presented by the researcher because of technical terms. But this can be resolved by researchers engaging the services of professional writers or communication officers to help with translating their study into more accessible language and share it widely with media outlets and the public.\nSharing results with the people who are most affected by them makes us better researchers and ensures that our work can be used to improve people's lives. Institutions and collaborators must recognise the value of doing so.", "50": "Research is meant to benefit society by raising public awareness and creating products and innovations that enhance development. For research to serve its full purpose, the results must leave the confines of research laboratories and academic journals.\nFindings effectively communicated can go a long way to serve the interests of the public. They can help address social injustices or improve treatments offered to patients.\nMany researchers seem to be content with sharing the results of their studies in academic journals or at conferences. But few journals allow everybody to read the findings. Even articles freely available are usually written in academic language incomprehensible to the average reader.\nFor researchers in the tenure-track system, their main goal is winning tenure, which in part can be achieved by getting a number of papers published in prominent journals. Pressures like this mean community-level outreach is not prioritised.\nMany researchers lack the writing skills to describe their results to a general audience. They may also worry about whether the public will understand their findings, or about findings being used to influence controversial policies. These concerns cause some researchers to shy away from communicating their findings outside the academic community.\nPropagating research findings beyond academic publications is particularly crucial for addressing certain social discrepancies. It can help families, communities, healthcare providers, policymakers, government agencies and other stakeholders to understand and respond to crises that plague society.\nThe benefits of sharing findings flow both ways. Engaging with other researchers and the public can lead to unexpected new connections and new ideas that could suggest fruitful new directions for research.\nTo benefit both researchers and the communities, the need to find innovative, accessible ways to share the work cannot be overstated.\nInstitutions and funding organisations should support more researchers to publish in open access journals so that the public doesn't have to pay to read them. Institutions and researchers should invest in partnerships that expand capacity for sharing results more broadly.\nFurthermore, ethics committees should make it mandatory for researchers to share their results with the public. Every research participant should opt in or out of receiving results, as part of the process of giving informed consent.\nThere could be misunderstanding of the findings presented by the researcher because of technical terms. But this can be resolved by researchers engaging the services of professional writers or communication officers to help with translating their study into more accessible language and share it widely with media outlets and the public.\nSharing results with the people who are most affected by them makes us better researchers and ensures that our work can be used to improve people's lives. Institutions and collaborators must recognise the value of doing so.", "51": "Spiders make their presence felt in late August and through early autumn. This is the mating season of some of the most common varieties, when male house spiders come out of hidden corners to look for females, and garden spiders reach adult size and spin their most dazzling webs.\nYet while the spider is a familiar fixture of nursery poems or songs and Halloween decorations, its relationship with humans is complicated. Fear of spiders is common and has serious impacts on the lives of sufferers. Its prevalence appears unrelated to any rational assessment of risk. Spiders in the UK are almost all harmless. Farmland species perform valuable ecosystem services, by preying on insects that are our competitors for crops. But they are a constant source of human anxieties—with a cultural association with witches and wickedness dating back to the middle ages.\nDoes this perhaps explain, in part, the lack of data about how spiders are faring in our age of ecological crisis? British butterflies are the most studied group of insects in the world, due to the long tradition of collecting and observing them. But spider conservationists point out that it was only in the 1980s that the classification of house spiders was properly sorted out. And while information about insect populations is gathered by experiments that measure the numbers hitting windscreens or traps, there have been few attempts to count spiders.\nThe huge reductions in the numbers of flying insects can only mean a reduction in spiders' food supply. A recent landmark study identified a 75% fall in insect populations between 1989 and 2016, with pesticide use thought to be to blame along with the destruction of wild areas for development. This means the overall picture for spiders is worrying, as it is for most creatures. But conservationists are most concerned about those varieties that are threatened due to habitat loss and fragmentation, which makes it impossible for them to migrate.\nOf around 650 spider species regularly recorded in the UK, the majority thrive in marshes and wasteland. Conservation efforts, often led by determined individuals, have helped some species to recover by reintroducing them to new areas. With rewilding now firmly on the environmental policy agenda, the hope is that in future, spiders will be enabled to migrate by themselves, adapting to climate change by moving along wildlife corridors.\nIt seems unlikely that spiders will ever attract the same level of human enthusiasm as bees, birds or butterflies, in spite of their unique status as nature's spinners. But as they reveal themselves in all their splendour this autumn, it would be a good thing if more animal lovers recognised the ways in which spiders are simply terrific.", "52": "Spiders make their presence felt in late August and through early autumn. This is the mating season of some of the most common varieties, when male house spiders come out of hidden corners to look for females, and garden spiders reach adult size and spin their most dazzling webs.\nYet while the spider is a familiar fixture of nursery poems or songs and Halloween decorations, its relationship with humans is complicated. Fear of spiders is common and has serious impacts on the lives of sufferers. Its prevalence appears unrelated to any rational assessment of risk. Spiders in the UK are almost all harmless. Farmland species perform valuable ecosystem services, by preying on insects that are our competitors for crops. But they are a constant source of human anxieties—with a cultural association with witches and wickedness dating back to the middle ages.\nDoes this perhaps explain, in part, the lack of data about how spiders are faring in our age of ecological crisis? British butterflies are the most studied group of insects in the world, due to the long tradition of collecting and observing them. But spider conservationists point out that it was only in the 1980s that the classification of house spiders was properly sorted out. And while information about insect populations is gathered by experiments that measure the numbers hitting windscreens or traps, there have been few attempts to count spiders.\nThe huge reductions in the numbers of flying insects can only mean a reduction in spiders' food supply. A recent landmark study identified a 75% fall in insect populations between 1989 and 2016, with pesticide use thought to be to blame along with the destruction of wild areas for development. This means the overall picture for spiders is worrying, as it is for most creatures. But conservationists are most concerned about those varieties that are threatened due to habitat loss and fragmentation, which makes it impossible for them to migrate.\nOf around 650 spider species regularly recorded in the UK, the majority thrive in marshes and wasteland. Conservation efforts, often led by determined individuals, have helped some species to recover by reintroducing them to new areas. With rewilding now firmly on the environmental policy agenda, the hope is that in future, spiders will be enabled to migrate by themselves, adapting to climate change by moving along wildlife corridors.\nIt seems unlikely that spiders will ever attract the same level of human enthusiasm as bees, birds or butterflies, in spite of their unique status as nature's spinners. But as they reveal themselves in all their splendour this autumn, it would be a good thing if more animal lovers recognised the ways in which spiders are simply terrific.", "53": "Spiders make their presence felt in late August and through early autumn. This is the mating season of some of the most common varieties, when male house spiders come out of hidden corners to look for females, and garden spiders reach adult size and spin their most dazzling webs.\nYet while the spider is a familiar fixture of nursery poems or songs and Halloween decorations, its relationship with humans is complicated. Fear of spiders is common and has serious impacts on the lives of sufferers. Its prevalence appears unrelated to any rational assessment of risk. Spiders in the UK are almost all harmless. Farmland species perform valuable ecosystem services, by preying on insects that are our competitors for crops. But they are a constant source of human anxieties—with a cultural association with witches and wickedness dating back to the middle ages.\nDoes this perhaps explain, in part, the lack of data about how spiders are faring in our age of ecological crisis? British butterflies are the most studied group of insects in the world, due to the long tradition of collecting and observing them. But spider conservationists point out that it was only in the 1980s that the classification of house spiders was properly sorted out. And while information about insect populations is gathered by experiments that measure the numbers hitting windscreens or traps, there have been few attempts to count spiders.\nThe huge reductions in the numbers of flying insects can only mean a reduction in spiders' food supply. A recent landmark study identified a 75% fall in insect populations between 1989 and 2016, with pesticide use thought to be to blame along with the destruction of wild areas for development. This means the overall picture for spiders is worrying, as it is for most creatures. But conservationists are most concerned about those varieties that are threatened due to habitat loss and fragmentation, which makes it impossible for them to migrate.\nOf around 650 spider species regularly recorded in the UK, the majority thrive in marshes and wasteland. Conservation efforts, often led by determined individuals, have helped some species to recover by reintroducing them to new areas. With rewilding now firmly on the environmental policy agenda, the hope is that in future, spiders will be enabled to migrate by themselves, adapting to climate change by moving along wildlife corridors.\nIt seems unlikely that spiders will ever attract the same level of human enthusiasm as bees, birds or butterflies, in spite of their unique status as nature's spinners. But as they reveal themselves in all their splendour this autumn, it would be a good thing if more animal lovers recognised the ways in which spiders are simply terrific.", "54": "Spiders make their presence felt in late August and through early autumn. This is the mating season of some of the most common varieties, when male house spiders come out of hidden corners to look for females, and garden spiders reach adult size and spin their most dazzling webs.\nYet while the spider is a familiar fixture of nursery poems or songs and Halloween decorations, its relationship with humans is complicated. Fear of spiders is common and has serious impacts on the lives of sufferers. Its prevalence appears unrelated to any rational assessment of risk. Spiders in the UK are almost all harmless. Farmland species perform valuable ecosystem services, by preying on insects that are our competitors for crops. But they are a constant source of human anxieties—with a cultural association with witches and wickedness dating back to the middle ages.\nDoes this perhaps explain, in part, the lack of data about how spiders are faring in our age of ecological crisis? British butterflies are the most studied group of insects in the world, due to the long tradition of collecting and observing them. But spider conservationists point out that it was only in the 1980s that the classification of house spiders was properly sorted out. And while information about insect populations is gathered by experiments that measure the numbers hitting windscreens or traps, there have been few attempts to count spiders.\nThe huge reductions in the numbers of flying insects can only mean a reduction in spiders' food supply. A recent landmark study identified a 75% fall in insect populations between 1989 and 2016, with pesticide use thought to be to blame along with the destruction of wild areas for development. This means the overall picture for spiders is worrying, as it is for most creatures. But conservationists are most concerned about those varieties that are threatened due to habitat loss and fragmentation, which makes it impossible for them to migrate.\nOf around 650 spider species regularly recorded in the UK, the majority thrive in marshes and wasteland. Conservation efforts, often led by determined individuals, have helped some species to recover by reintroducing them to new areas. With rewilding now firmly on the environmental policy agenda, the hope is that in future, spiders will be enabled to migrate by themselves, adapting to climate change by moving along wildlife corridors.\nIt seems unlikely that spiders will ever attract the same level of human enthusiasm as bees, birds or butterflies, in spite of their unique status as nature's spinners. But as they reveal themselves in all their splendour this autumn, it would be a good thing if more animal lovers recognised the ways in which spiders are simply terrific.", "55": "Spiders make their presence felt in late August and through early autumn. This is the mating season of some of the most common varieties, when male house spiders come out of hidden corners to look for females, and garden spiders reach adult size and spin their most dazzling webs.\nYet while the spider is a familiar fixture of nursery poems or songs and Halloween decorations, its relationship with humans is complicated. Fear of spiders is common and has serious impacts on the lives of sufferers. Its prevalence appears unrelated to any rational assessment of risk. Spiders in the UK are almost all harmless. Farmland species perform valuable ecosystem services, by preying on insects that are our competitors for crops. But they are a constant source of human anxieties—with a cultural association with witches and wickedness dating back to the middle ages.\nDoes this perhaps explain, in part, the lack of data about how spiders are faring in our age of ecological crisis? British butterflies are the most studied group of insects in the world, due to the long tradition of collecting and observing them. But spider conservationists point out that it was only in the 1980s that the classification of house spiders was properly sorted out. And while information about insect populations is gathered by experiments that measure the numbers hitting windscreens or traps, there have been few attempts to count spiders.\nThe huge reductions in the numbers of flying insects can only mean a reduction in spiders' food supply. A recent landmark study identified a 75% fall in insect populations between 1989 and 2016, with pesticide use thought to be to blame along with the destruction of wild areas for development. This means the overall picture for spiders is worrying, as it is for most creatures. But conservationists are most concerned about those varieties that are threatened due to habitat loss and fragmentation, which makes it impossible for them to migrate.\nOf around 650 spider species regularly recorded in the UK, the majority thrive in marshes and wasteland. Conservation efforts, often led by determined individuals, have helped some species to recover by reintroducing them to new areas. With rewilding now firmly on the environmental policy agenda, the hope is that in future, spiders will be enabled to migrate by themselves, adapting to climate change by moving along wildlife corridors.\nIt seems unlikely that spiders will ever attract the same level of human enthusiasm as bees, birds or butterflies, in spite of their unique status as nature's spinners. But as they reveal themselves in all their splendour this autumn, it would be a good thing if more animal lovers recognised the ways in which spiders are simply terrific."}, "questions": [{"id": "2023-12-3-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) correlation", "B) dense", "C) deter", "D) equity", "E) foster", "F) imperative", "G) impose", "H) irrespective", "I) longevity", "J) navigate", "K) recreational", "L) rotten", "M) sprawl", "N) vibrate", "O) vicinity"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) impose\n动词\n【词性槽】also 后并列谓语缺动词\n【依据】impose costs 是固定地道搭配\nimpose costs/burden/restrictions on… 是英语里高频搭配，表\"强加、招致（成本/负\n担）\"。本句用 but also 把\"带来好处\"与\"招致健康代价\"形成转折并列：provide great\nbenefits but also __(26)__ health costs，impose 在 cost 前正是最自然的动词。\n【竞争词】J) navigate \"导航健康代价\"搭配不成立\nnavigate 不与 costs 搭配；意义上也无法表达\"招致\"。\nN) vibrate 义为\"振动\"，与抽象 health costs 不搭\nvibrate 是物理动词，与抽象代价宾语无法搭配。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·also 后并列谓语缺动词"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) correlation", "B) dense", "C) deter", "D) equity", "E) foster", "F) imperative", "G) impose", "H) irrespective", "I) longevity", "J) navigate", "K) recreational", "L) rotten", "M) sprawl", "N) vibrate", "O) vicinity"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) sprawl\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词 urban 后需名词作主语\n【依据】urban sprawl 是地道术语，对应\"城市蔓延招致开车多+肥胖\"\nurban sprawl（城市无序扩张）是城市规划/健康研究里高度固定的术语，指城市低密度向\n外摊大饼。摊得越大，居民越依赖开车、越少步行，自然 encourages more driving and\nis associated with heavier weight——因果链严丝合缝。\n【竞争词】A) correlation 下一题 28 才需要 correlation\ncorrelation 多表\"相关性\"，更适合下句 This __(28)__ suggests that… 指代\"上一句揭示的相关\n性\"，留给 28 题更恰当；而且 urban correlation 也不是常见说法。\nD) equity \"城市公平鼓励开车\"逻辑荒诞\nequity 本意\"公平\"，与\"鼓励更多开车、体重更重\"的负面后果在逻辑上矛盾。34 题的 promote\n___ 才是它的归宿。\nI) longevity \"城市寿命鼓励开车\"语义不通\nlongevity \"长寿\" 与 \"鼓励开车、体重增加\" 的负面因果对不上；32 题的 studies of ___ 才与它\n匹配。\nO) vicinity \"城市附近鼓励开车\"指代不清\nvicinity 指\"附近地区\"，多用于 in the vicinity of 短语，单独作 \"urban + vicinity\" 搭配不成\n立，也无法解释\"鼓励开车\"。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 O) —— vicinity 看着也能配 urban。urban + 名词的位置最容易被\nvicinity 干扰——\"城市的附近地区\"似通非通。但比对学科术语 urban sprawl 才是城市规\n划的固定说法，且后文\"鼓励开车/体重增加\"只能由\"无序扩张\"解释。防坑句：拿不准就回看\n后续 encourages more driving 因果链是否成立。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·形容词 urban 后需名词作主语"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) correlation", "B) dense", "C) deter", "D) equity", "E) foster", "F) imperative", "G) impose", "H) irrespective", "I) longevity", "J) navigate", "K) recreational", "L) rotten", "M) sprawl", "N) vibrate", "O) vicinity"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) correlation\n名词\n【词性槽】指示词 This 后需单数名词\n【依据】回指上句\"扩张→开车多→更胖\"的相关性\n上一句 Recent studies show that urban __(27)__ encourages more driving and is\nassociated with heavier weight. 描述的就是\"城市蔓延\"与\"体重\"之间的关联——is\nassociated with 的标准说法正是 correlation。本句用 This correlation 回指，紧扣\"相关\n性\"语义。\n【竞争词】D) equity \"这种公平\"无回指对象\n上句没有讨论\"公平\"，equity 在此找不到回指意义；语段焦点是\"扩张和体重的关联\"。\nI) longevity 上句谈\"体重增加\"不谈\"寿命\"\nlongevity \"长寿\" 与上句\"鼓励开车、体重增加\"语义对不上号。\nO) vicinity vicinity 表地点，不指代研究结论\nThis vicinity 只能指\"这一带\"，无法回指上句揭示的关系。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·指示词 This 后需单数名词"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) correlation", "B) dense", "C) deter", "D) equity", "E) foster", "F) imperative", "G) impose", "H) irrespective", "I) longevity", "J) navigate", "K) recreational", "L) rotten", "M) sprawl", "N) vibrate", "O) vicinity"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) imperative\n形容词\n【词性槽】it is ___ that 句型中需形容词作表语\n【依据】\"城市规划者必须铭记这一点至关重要\"\nimperative \"至关重要的、势在必行的\" 恰好是 it is imperative that 的高频搭配，并且\nthat 从句里 bear this in mind 用了 bear（虚拟语气的原形动词），正与 imperative 引导\n的虚拟语气句型对得上。语义上承接上文 Recent studies show that urban __(27)__\nencourages more driving and is associated with heavier weight.，把\"城市规划要顾及\n健康\"提到一个必须做的高度。\n【竞争词】H) irrespective 不用于 it is ___ that 句型\nirrespective 只能搭配 of，不进入 \"it is + adj. + that\" 句型，结构上排除。\nL) rotten 色彩与作者立场相反\n作者整段在呼吁城市规划者重视健康，是正面倡导；代入 it is rotten that…（\"这件事真糟糕\"）\n情感色彩完全相反。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·it is ___ that 句型中需形"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) correlation", "B) dense", "C) deter", "D) equity", "E) foster", "F) imperative", "G) impose", "H) irrespective", "I) longevity", "J) navigate", "K) recreational", "L) rotten", "M) sprawl", "N) vibrate", "O) vicinity"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) foster\n动词\n【词性槽】情态动词 need to 后需动词原形\n【依据】\"推行/促进标准公共卫生做法\"\nfoster 表\"促进、推行、培育\"，与 standard public health practices 搭配恰好表达\"城市仍\n需推行/促进标准公共卫生做法\"。本句承接 urban planning still centers in large part on\nsolving the problems of the past，foster 体现\"继续保留并落实传统做法\"的正面动作。\n【竞争词】C) deter \"阻止公共卫生做法\"与全文立场相反\ndeter 表\"阻止\"，城市不可能去\"阻止\"公共卫生措施；它的归宿在 35 题的\"deter…from\njoining\"句式里。\nG) impose impose 强调\"强加\"，色彩偏重 + 留给 26 题用\nimpose 多与 burden/cost/tax/restriction 搭配，强调强行加诸；与 standard public health\npractices 的正面语义不搭。26 题的 impose health costs（招致健康代价）是它的真正归宿。\nJ) navigate \"导航公共卫生做法\"搭配不通\nnavigate 指\"穿行、操纵\"，常配 navigate the system/streets，不与 practices 直接搭配表\n示\"推行\"。\nN) vibrate 义为\"振动\"，与公共卫生主题南辕北辙\nvibrate 是物理动作，与抽象的 practices 完全无法搭配。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 G) —— impose 也能后接抽象宾语。动词空填 impose 看似可行\n（\"施加公共卫生做法\"似乎也通），但 impose 色彩偏\"强加、施压\"，与 standard public\nhealth practices 的正面/中性语义不合；况且 26 题的 impose health costs 才是 impose\n真正的归宿。防坑句：动词色彩要与宾语色彩一致。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·情态动词 need to 后需动词原形"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) correlation", "B) dense", "C) deter", "D) equity", "E) foster", "F) imperative", "G) impose", "H) irrespective", "I) longevity", "J) navigate", "K) recreational", "L) rotten", "M) sprawl", "N) vibrate", "O) vicinity"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) dense\n形容词\n【词性槽】名词 areas 前需形容词作定语\n【依据】\"限制重型卡车穿越人口稠密的居民区\"语义最顺\n句子讲的是公共健康措施——把有害设施与住宅隔开、限制重卡穿行居民区。能让\"限制重\n卡\"具有现实意义的形容词，最自然的就是\"人口稠密的\"：人越密，重卡穿行的健康风险越\n大。代入后 restricting heavy truck traffic through __(31)__ residential areas 读作\ndense residential areas，搭配地道、语义贴。\n【竞争词】F) imperative 义为\"至关重要的\"，与 residential areas 语义不搭\nimperative 通常修饰任务、需求（it is imperative that…），不用来形容\"居民区\"的物理属性；\n而且后面 29 题正需要 it is imperative that… 的句式，留给那里更合适。\nH) irrespective 几乎只用作 irrespective of 短语，做后置状语\nirrespective 几乎不放在名词前作前置定语，本句也没有 of 引出的比较对象，结构上就用不了。\nK) recreational \"娱乐的居民区\"语义不通\nrecreational 通常修饰 spaces、activities、facilities；与 residential 一起说\"娱乐的居民区\"逻\n辑奇怪。况且 33 题的 more __(33)__ spaces 正等着 recreational。\nL) rotten 色彩太负面，超出此处中性描述\nrotten 强调\"腐败\"\"糟糕透顶\"，色彩极重；文章只是中性地讲\"哪些居民区要保护\"，没有要把这\n些社区贬为\"破败\"之意。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·名词 areas 前需形容词作定语"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) correlation", "B) dense", "C) deter", "D) equity", "E) foster", "F) imperative", "G) impose", "H) irrespective", "I) longevity", "J) navigate", "K) recreational", "L) rotten", "M) sprawl", "N) vibrate", "O) vicinity"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) longevity\n名词\n【词性槽】介词 of 后需名词作宾语\n【依据】\"长寿研究\"与 live the longest 完美呼应\nthat 从句 people live the longest in environments where physical activity is part of\neveryday life 中 live the longest 直接对应 longevity（长寿）。\"长寿研究\"自然得出\"在日\n常活动的环境里活得最久\"。\n【竞争词】D) equity \"公平研究\"与活得最久无关\nequity 与 live the longest 没有语义指向；equity 留给 34 题正合适。\nO) vicinity \"附近地区研究\"语义空泛\nstudies of vicinity 不是常见搭配，也不指向\"寿命/活得久\"这个研究结论。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·介词 of 后需名词作宾语"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) correlation", "B) dense", "C) deter", "D) equity", "E) foster", "F) imperative", "G) impose", "H) irrespective", "I) longevity", "J) navigate", "K) recreational", "L) rotten", "M) sprawl", "N) vibrate", "O) vicinity"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) recreational\n形容词\n【词性槽】形容词 more 与名词 spaces 之间需形容词\n【依据】\"更多娱乐空间\"呼应\"鼓励市民活动\"\n第三段在讲\"提供促进活动的城市要素\"，与 walkable spaces（步行空间）、protected\nbike lanes（受保护的自行车道）并列的第三项理应是同一逻辑的活动空间。recreational\nspaces \"娱乐/休闲空间\" 正好契合：让人有地方运动、玩耍，呼应 encouraging physical\nactivity by making it easier and safer for people to recreate, walk, bike and take\npublic transportation 里的 recreate。\n【竞争词】F) imperative 义\"必要的\"，不用来描述空间属性\nimperative spaces 不是英语里的常见搭配，且把\"空间\"说成\"势在必行的\"语义不通。\nH) irrespective 几乎只用于 irrespective of 短语\n不能作前置定语，结构上排除。\nL) rotten 贬义\"破败\"，与正面措施清单矛盾\n本段在列\"重要的良性改造\"，提供\"破败空间\"显然不是 important steps。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·形容词 more 与名词 spaces"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) correlation", "B) dense", "C) deter", "D) equity", "E) foster", "F) imperative", "G) impose", "H) irrespective", "I) longevity", "J) navigate", "K) recreational", "L) rotten", "M) sprawl", "N) vibrate", "O) vicinity"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) equity\n名词\n【词性槽】动词 promotes 后需名词作宾语\n【依据】\"促进公平\"恰是与昂贵私人健身房对立面\nrather than 后的反面情境是 private gyms with often-expensive monthly fees that\n__(35)__ less wealthy people from joining——昂贵会员费把低收入者排除在外，本质\n是\"不公平\"。promotes equity \"促进公平\"正是它的反面，并紧扣公共空间面向所有人这一\n公共健康主旨。\n【竞争词】O) vicinity \"促进附近地区\"语义混乱\nvicinity 表\"附近\"，promotes vicinity 不成搭配，也无法对应 rather than 后\"将穷人挡在门\n外\"的不公平反面。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·动词 promotes 后需名词作宾语"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) correlation", "B) dense", "C) deter", "D) equity", "E) foster", "F) imperative", "G) impose", "H) irrespective", "I) longevity", "J) navigate", "K) recreational", "L) rotten", "M) sprawl", "N) vibrate", "O) vicinity"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) deter\n动词\n【词性槽】that 引导的定语从句缺谓语\n【依据】deter sb. from doing 是固定搭配\ndeter \"使打消念头/阻止\"，固定搭配 deter sb. from doing sth.。代入恰好表达\"昂贵会员\n费让低收入者打消加入健身房的念头\"，与 34 题的 equity 形成正反对照，把\"私人健身房不\n公平\"这层意思讲透。\n【竞争词】G) impose impose 不跟 sb. from doing 结构\nimpose sth. on sb. 是其正确搭配，不能说 impose sb. from doing。26 题才是它的归宿。\nJ) navigate navigate 不带 from doing 结构\nnavigate 是\"穿行、操纵\"，与\"阻止某人做某事\"语义和结构都不匹配。\nN) vibrate 义为\"振动\"，与抽象的\"阻止加入\"无关\nvibrate 是物理动词，无法承接 sb. from doing。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·that 引导的定语从句缺谓语"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Research indicates that brain training is likely to boost one's ability at specific tasks, but not one's other cognitive abilities.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】brain training 与任务特异性\n【定位】However, research suggests that although this type of training may improve one's abilities at\nthe tasks themselves, they don't seem to improve other abilities. In other words, practicing a\nletter-detection task will, over time, improve your letter-detection skills, but it will not\nnecessarily enhance your other perceptual abilities.\n然而，研究表明，尽管这类训练可能会提升一个人在被训练任务本身上的能力，但它们似乎\n并不能提升其他能力。换言之，长期练习字母检测任务会提高你的字母检测技能，但未必能\n增强你其他的感知能力。\n【改写】brain training ↔ train your brain\n名词化的同义复现\nboost one's ability at specific tasks ↔ improve one's abilities at the tasks themselves\n同义动词替换\nnot one's other cognitive abilities ↔ they don't seem to improve other abilities\n否定面对应\n【辨邻】 C C 段的结论落在 in reality the brain is not a muscle，并没有讨论训练是否能迁移到\n其他能力，因此不是 Q36 的源头段。\nIt is important to exercise your brain", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "According to estimates, the number of people who speak two languages or more is greater than those who speak one language only.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】数量估计：双语者多于单语者\n【定位】In fact, it has been estimated that there are fewer monolingual speakers in the world than\nbilinguals and multilinguals.\n事实上，据估计，世界上单语使用者的数量少于双语和多语使用者。\n【改写】According to estimates ↔ it has been estimated that\n估计的同义表达\nthe number of people who speak two languages or more is greater than those who speak one\nlanguage only\n↔ there are fewer monolingual speakers in the world than bilinguals and multilinguals\n比较关系翻面", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "For the time being, we do not know what causes people to lose their cognitive abilities, or what we can do to prevent it.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】成因未明与预防无解的双重否定\n【定位】At this time, the causes of the disease are not well understood, and consequently, there are no\nproven steps that people can take to prevent it.\n目前，该病的成因尚不清楚，因此，人们没有任何经证实的步骤可以用来预防它。\n【改写】we do not know what causes people to lose their cognitive abilities ↔\nthe causes of the disease are not well understood\n上位词指代 + 同义否定\nwhat we can do to prevent it ↔ there are no proven steps that people can take to prevent it\n否定表达的转写\n【辨邻】 J J 段的「原因不明」指向 why differences between bilinguals and monolinguals\nexist，并不指向痴呆病因或其预防，因此不能匹配 Q38。\nthey still have not established exactly how or why differences between bilinguals and\nmonolinguals exist", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "It is hard to determine whether people who are free from dementia tend to have more social activities, or more social activities keep people away from dementia.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】社交活动与痴呆的因果方向\n【定位】So we can't really say whether being socially active prevents the onset of dementia, or if\npeople who don't have dementia are more likely to be socially active.\n因此，我们无法真正确定，是社交活跃预防了痴呆的发生，还是没有痴呆的人更倾向于社交\n活跃。\n【改写】people who are free from dementia tend to have more social activities ↔\npeople who don't have dementia are more likely to be socially active\n同义短语对换\nmore social activities keep people away from dementia ↔\nbeing socially active prevents the onset of dementia\n动词的同义替换", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "There is evidence that learning foreign languages might be beneficial to boosting one's cognitive abilities.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】学外语对认知有益的证据\n【定位】However, there is some reason to believe that learning languages might be different. The best\nevidence that foreign language learning confers cognitive benefits comes from research with\nthose who are already bilingual.\n然而，我们有理由相信学习语言可能不一样。证明外语学习能带来认知收益的最佳证据，来\n自对那些已经掌握双语者的研究。\n【改写】There is evidence ↔ The best evidence\n存在性陈述的弱化\nlearning foreign languages might be beneficial to boosting one's cognitive abilities ↔\nforeign language learning confers cognitive benefits\n名词化与动名化的双向转换\n【辨邻】 G G 段的核心句 bilingual individuals outperform their monolingual counterparts\non a variety of cognitive tasks 是结论本身，与 There is evidence 强调的「证据存\n在」层级不同，因此不是源头段。\nbilingual individuals outperform their monolingual counterparts on a variety of cognitive tasks,\nsuch as following complex instructions, and switching to new instructions\nH H 段的证据落在 received their diagnosis at 75.5 years 这一发病年龄数据上，而非\n笼统的「认知能力提升」，因此不能匹配 Q40。\nthere is evidence to support this claim", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "It was suggested that only those who always spoke two languages could benefit from bilingualism.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】始终使用双语才受益\n【定位】Crucially, Bialystok suggested that the positive benefits of being bilingual were only found in\nthose who used both languages all the time.\n至关重要的是，Bialystok 指出，双语带来的积极益处只出现在那些一直使用两种语言的人\n身上。\n【改写】It was suggested ↔ Bialystok suggested\n主动转被动\nonly those who always spoke two languages ↔\nonly found in those who used both languages all the time\n限定词同义对位\nbenefit from bilingualism ↔ the positive benefits of being bilingual\n动名互换\n【辨邻】 H H 段的论点是 a difference of four years is highly significant，并未说「只有一直用\n双语的人才受益」，而 only/always 这一限定恰恰是 Q41 的核心，所以排除 H。\nThe psychologist Ellen Bialystok and her colleagues obtained the histories of 184 individuals who\nhad made use of a memory clinic in Toronto", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "The brain is different from muscles in that it keeps working even when the body is at rest.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】大脑休息时仍工作\n【定位】Unlike muscles, the brain is always active and works even during periods of rest and sleep.\n与肌肉不同，大脑始终在活动，即使在休息和睡眠时也在工作。\n【改写】The brain is different from muscles ↔ Unlike muscles, the brain\n对照短语的同义替换\nit keeps working even when the body is at rest ↔\nthe brain is always active and works even during periods of rest and sleep\n动作与时间的同义对应\n【辨邻】 D D 段的关键句 if the brain is not a muscle, can it still be exercised? 只是承接上\n文，未承载 keeps working even when the body is at rest 这一信息。\nWhile it makes for a colorful analogy, comparing the brain to a muscle is inaccurate and misleading", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "People who speak two languages do better at a number of cognitive tasks than those who speak only one language.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】双语者在多项认知任务上胜过单语者\n【定位】In fact, bilingual individuals outperform their monolingual counterparts on a variety of\ncognitive tasks, such as following complex instructions, and switching to new instructions.\n事实上，双语者在多项认知任务上的表现都胜过他们的单语对应者，比如遵循复杂指令、切\n换到新指令。\n【改写】People who speak two languages ↔ bilingual individuals\n解释性短语 vs 术语\ndo better at a number of cognitive tasks ↔\noutperform their monolingual counterparts on a variety of cognitive tasks\n比较动词与量词的同义\nthose who speak only one language ↔ monolingual counterparts\n解释性短语 vs 术语\n【辨邻】 H H 段的比较结果是 received their diagnosis at 75.5 years, on average，并未涉及\n「多项认知任务上谁更好」，因此不是源头段。\nthe monolinguals in the sample had an average age of 71.4 years at time of onset\nI I 段核心句 bilingual patients developed symptoms of dementia 4.5 years later\nthan monolinguals 不承载 a number of cognitive tasks 这一信息。\nbilingual patients developed symptoms of dementia 4.5 years later than monolinguals", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Dementia is different from being merely forgetful and entails more serious trouble.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】痴呆不同于普通健忘\n【定位】It is also important to remember that dementia is not the same thing as normal forgetfulness.\nPeople with dementia have more serious problems, like feeling confused or getting lost in a\nfamiliar place.\n同样重要的是要记住，痴呆并不等同于正常的健忘。痴呆患者会遇到更严重的问题，比如感\n到困惑或在熟悉的地方迷路。\n【改写】Dementia is different from being merely forgetful ↔\ndementia is not the same thing as normal forgetfulness\n否定等值\nentails more serious trouble ↔ have more serious problems\n动词与名词的同义替换", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "It is claimed that more monolinguals suffer from Alzheimer's disease than bilinguals.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】单语者中阿尔茨海默氏病人更多\n【定位】If the benefits of being bilingual spill over to other aspects of cognition, then we would\nexpect to see a lower incidence of Alzheimer's disease in bilinguals than in monolinguals,\nor at least a later onset of Alzheimer's for bilinguals. In fact, there is evidence to support\nthis claim.\n如果双语带来的好处可以外溢到其他认知层面，那么我们就可能预期双语者中阿尔茨海默\n氏病的发病率低于单语者，或者至少双语者发病时间更晚。事实上，确实有证据支持这一\n主张。\n【改写】It is claimed ↔ there is evidence to support this claim\nclaim 同根词对应\nmore monolinguals suffer from Alzheimer's disease than bilinguals ↔\na lower incidence of Alzheimer's disease in bilinguals than in monolinguals\n发病率比较翻面\n【辨邻】 I I 段的核心句 bilingual patients developed symptoms of dementia 4.5 years later\nthan monolinguals 强调发病时间差，并不直接承载 more monolinguals suffer\nfrom Alzheimer's disease 这一发病人数或发病率的比较。\nbilingual patients developed symptoms of dementia 4.5 years later than monolinguals\nPARAGRAPH MAP · 段落主旨图\n个段落各讲了什么\n逐段一句主旨 + 该段最具区分度的「信息指纹」，是逐句回扫定位的工作台。\nA 引出全文话题：学外语可能延缓痴呆症的发病；同时指出目前还不清楚阿尔茨海默氏\n病的成因，也没有可证实的预防办法。\n信息指纹 病因未明与预防无解\nB 厘清对痴呆与大脑老化的常见误解；强调痴呆并非正常衰老的必然结果，也不同于一\n般的健忘。\n信息指纹 痴呆与健忘的区别\nC 把大脑比作肌肉只是形象比喻；事实上大脑不是肌肉——它永远在工作，脑细胞终生\n存在并不断新生。\n信息指纹 脑非肌肉、休息时仍工作\nD 讨论大脑能否被训练；研究表明所谓脑力训练可以提升被训练任务本身的表现，但难\n以迁移到其他认知能力。\n信息指纹 脑训练只对特定任务有效\nE 把镜头转向外语学习：来自双语者的研究提供了外语学习能带来认知收益的最佳\n证据。\n信息指纹 双语研究最具说服力的证据来源\nF 双语和多语现象比想象中普遍；以瑞士四种官方语言和非洲多语并用作例。\n信息指纹 双语者多于单语者的估算\nG\n双语者在多任务处理上更出色；在多项认知任务上胜过单语者，但词汇量和取词速度\n上有小幅劣势。\n信息指纹 双语者多项认知任务胜过单语者\nH\n通过加拿大多伦多记忆诊所对 184 人的研究，发现双语者的阿尔茨海默氏病平均发病\n年龄比单语者晚约四年。\n信息指纹 多伦多 184 人四年差研究\nI\n印度的另一项研究得出类似结论——双语患者发病晚 4.5 年；并指出真正受益于双语\n的是那些一直同时使用两种语言的人。\n信息指纹 印度研究 4.5 年差与坚持双语\nJ\n提醒上述研究仅能说明两组之间有差异，并未解释差异产生的原因，需进一步研究。\n信息指纹 差异已被观察但成因待查\nK 讨论社交活动与延缓痴呆的关系：因果方向无法确定——是社交活动预防了痴呆，还\n是没痴呆的人更愿意社交。\n信息指纹 社交与痴呆的因果不明\nL 结尾退一步：即便学外语不是灵丹妙药，至少能让你成为一个更好的外语使用者，本\n身就有诸多益处。\n信息指纹 外语学习本身就有益", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "How can research serve its full purpose according to the author?", "options": ["A) With researchers being aware of public interests.", "B) With its findings published in prominent journals.", "C) With researchers creating products that enhance social development.", "D) With its findings properly communicated beyond the academic circle."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】How can research serve its full purpose according to the author? 问的是\"通过什么方\n式\"，要求从文中找到作者明示的条件句。\n【拆句】题干 How can research serve its full purpose according to the author? 是一个标准\nHow 类疑问句：情态动词 can 提到主语 research 前，谓语 serve 带宾语 its full\npurpose；according to the author 是状语，只是限定信息来源，不影响理解。结构平铺\n直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】For research to serve its full purpose, the results must leave the confines of research\nlaboratories and academic journals.\n要让研究充分发挥其作用，研究结果必须走出实验室和学术期刊的范围。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nWith researchers being aware of public interests 说\"研究者要意识到公众利益\"，原\n文从未谈论\"研究者的觉察\"这一前提，P1 强调的是\"传播\"而非\"觉察\"。\nB) 与原文相反\nWith its findings published in prominent journals 把\"发表在顶级期刊上\"当作答案，\n但 P1 恰恰指出\"必须离开学术期刊\"——发表在顶级期刊只是 P4 中讲到的研究者评职称\n的手段，与\"研究发挥充分作用\"相反。\nC) 张冠李戴\nWith researchers creating products that enhance social development 把 creating\nproducts and innovations that enhance development 这句\"研究的目标之一\"挪用来\n作为\"研究发挥作用的方式\"——这是研究的目的描述，不是 serve its full purpose 的\n条件。\nD) ✓ 正确\nthe results must leave the confines of research laboratories and academic journals ↔\nWith its findings properly communicated beyond the academic circle\n选项 With its findings properly communicated beyond the academic circle 用同义\n替换还原了预判的\"研究结果必须走出学术圈\"，是定位句的标准转述。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "Why do ordinary readers find it difficult to access the results of researchers' studies?", "options": ["A) They cannot understand the academic language used for reporting these results.", "B) They feel intimidated by the jargon researchers use to describe their findings.", "C) They do not attend conferences where these results are freely available.", "D) They have few chances to locate the journals that publish these findings."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】Why do ordinary readers find it difficult to access the results of researchers' studies?\n问具体原因，且原因点是\"读不懂研究结果\"这一具体行为，需回原文找直接陈述。\n【拆句】题干 Why do ordinary readers find it difficult to access the results of researchers'\nstudies? 主干是 readers find it difficult to access，find sth difficult to do 是常见结\n构，it 是形式宾语，真正宾语是后面的不定式 to access...；the results of researchers'\nstudies 是多重 of 修饰的名词短语。无难点。\n【定位】Even articles freely available are usually written in academic language incomprehensible to\nthe average reader.\n即便那些免费可读的文章，通常也是用普通读者无法理解的学术语言写成。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nacademic language incomprehensible to the average reader ↔\nThey cannot understand the academic language used for reporting these results\n选项 They cannot understand the academic language used for reporting these\nresults 是定位句\"X 对 Y 不可理解\"到\"Y 不能理解 X\"的标准视角转换，与预判完全\n吻合。\nB) 无中生有\nThey feel intimidated by the jargon researchers use to describe their findings 中\nfeel intimidated（感到畏惧）原文从未提及；jargon（行话）原文也未出现这个词。\nC) 偷换概念\nThey do not attend conferences where these results are freely available 把 freely\navailable 从\"免费可读\"偷换为\"会议上免费提供\"；原文是说\"少数期刊允许人人阅读，即\n便免费可读也用艰涩语言\"，不是说在 conferences 上免费提供。\nD) 答非所问\nThey have few chances to locate the journals that publish these findings 说\"普通\n读者找不到这些期刊\"，但原文 But few journals allow everybody to read the\nfindings 说的是\"少数期刊允许所有人阅读\"——即\"读到\"的问题，而非\"找到\"的问题；\n且题干问的是 difficult to access the results（读懂结果），不是 difficult to locate\nthe journals（找到期刊）。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What is one of the reasons some researchers won't prioritise communicating their findings to the public?", "options": ["A) They can thrive on the papers published.", "B) Their top consideration is to win tenure", "C) Their main goal is gaining recognition in their field.", "D) They have to struggle to reach out to the community."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What is one of the reasons some researchers won't prioritise communicating their\nfindings to the public? 直接问\"原因之一\"，要求从原文找明示因果句。\n【拆句】题干 What is one of the reasons some researchers won't prioritise communicating\ntheir findings to the public? 主干是 What is one of the reasons...；reasons 后的 some\nresearchers won't prioritise... 是省略 that 的定语从句修饰 reasons；communicating 后\n的 their findings to the public 是 V-ing 短语的双宾语结构。结构略长但语义清晰。\n【定位】For researchers in the tenure-track system, their main goal is winning tenure, which in part\ncan be achieved by getting a number of papers published in prominent journals. Pressures like\nthis mean community-level outreach is not prioritised.\n对处在终身教职轨制度中的研究者来说，他们的主要目标是获得终身教职，而获得终身教职\n部分可以通过在顶级期刊上发表若干论文实现。诸如此类的压力意味着面向社区层面的对外\n传播不被优先考虑。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\nThey can thrive on the papers published 说\"他们靠发表的论文茁壮成长\"——题干问\n的是\"为什么不优先对外传播的原因\"，A 描述的是\"发文的结果/好处\"，而非\"不愿对外传\n播的原因\"，且 thrive on 的积极语义与原文\"压力\"对应不上。\nB) ✓ 正确\ntheir main goal is winning tenure ↔ Their top consideration is to win tenure\n选项 Their top consideration is to win tenure 是定位句 their main goal is winning\ntenure 几乎逐词的同义替换，且与预判\"首要目标是 tenure\"完全一致。\nC) 无中生有\nTheir main goal is gaining recognition in their field 把研究者目标改成\"在领域内获\n得认可\"——原文 their main goal is winning tenure 明确写的是 winning tenure（拿\n到终身教职），不是 recognition；这是把因果中段的\"靠发文\"过度泛化为\"求认可\"。\nD) 与原文相反\nThey have to struggle to reach out to the community 说\"他们必须努力对外传播\"，\n但原文 community-level outreach is not prioritised 是说\"不优先做\"——不是\"努力\n做\"，方向完全相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "How can sharing findings benefit researchers themselves?", "options": ["A) By helping them to identify new research directions.", "B) By enabling them to understand crises plaguing society.", "C) By enabling them to effectively address social discrepancies.", "D) By helping them to forge ties with government agencies."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】How can sharing findings benefit researchers themselves? 问\"如何受益\"，需要在原文\n找具体受益机制的陈述。\n【拆句】题干 How can sharing findings benefit researchers themselves? 主干 sharing findings\n作主语（动名词短语），benefit 是及物动词，researchers themselves 是宾语+反身代词\n强调。themselves 是理解关键，标明\"受益方是研究者自己\"。\n【定位】Engaging with other researchers and the public can lead to unexpected new connections and\nnew ideas that could suggest fruitful new directions for research.\n与其他研究者及公众的互动可能催生意想不到的新联系和新想法，进而可能指明富有成果的\n新研究方向。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\ncould suggest fruitful new directions for research ↔\nBy helping them to identify new research directions\n选项 By helping them to identify new research directions 是定位句 could suggest\nfruitful new directions for research 的同义改写，且 By Ving 结构正好回应题\n干 How。\nB) 张冠李戴\nBy enabling them to understand crises plaguing society 借用了 respond to crises\nthat plague society 的措辞——但 P6 讲的是\"分享有助于其他主体（家庭、社区、政\n策制定者等）理解危机\"，受益者并非\"研究者自身\"；与题干 researchers themselves\n错位。\nC) 张冠李戴\nBy enabling them to effectively address social discrepancies 借用 addressing\ncertain social discrepancies 的措辞——同样是 P6 讲对社会的好处，而非对研究者自\n身的好处；与题干 themselves 错位。\nD) 无中生有\nBy helping them to forge ties with government agencies 说\"帮助他们与政府机构建\n立关系\"——原文虽提过 government agencies（P6 列举受益方时），但从未把\"与政\n府机构结盟\"作为研究者自身的受益方式。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "Why are researchers advised to engage the services of professional writers or communication officers?", "options": ["A) To satisfy ethics committees' mandatory requirements of researchers.", "B) To translate their study into languages accessible to readers overseas.", "C) To make their publications correctly understood by the public.", "D) To render their findings acceptable by prominent journals."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】Why are researchers advised to engage the services of professional writers or\ncommunication officers? 问\"为何建议聘请\"，需要从原文找明示的目的或结果状语。\n【拆句】题干 Why are researchers advised to engage the services of professional writers or\ncommunication officers? 是被动语态：are advised to do（被建议做）；engage 在此为\n正式词义\"聘请\"；professional writers or communication officers 是并列名词词组。结构\n清晰，注意 engage 不是\"参与\"而是\"聘用\"。\n【定位】But this can be resolved by researchers engaging the services of professional writers or\ncommunication officers to help with translating their study into more accessible language and\nshare it widely with media outlets and the public.\n但这可以通过研究者聘请专业写手或传播官来解决，让他们帮助把研究\"翻译\"成更通俗易懂\n的语言，并广泛地向媒体和公众分享。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nTo satisfy ethics committees' mandatory requirements of researchers 把 ethics\ncommittees should make it mandatory 这一伦理委员会的强制要求挪用来作为聘请\n写手的目的；但 P10 与 P11 是两个独立建议——伦理委员会强制研究者分享是一回\n事，聘请写手帮助翻译是另一回事，两者目的不同。\nB) 偷换概念\nTo translate their study into languages accessible to readers overseas 把 more\naccessible language 偷换为 languages accessible to readers overseas（让海外读者\n读懂）——原文从未提及 overseas / readers abroad 等概念，只说\"通俗易懂\"，不涉\n及外语翻译。\nC) ✓ 正确\nto help with translating their study into more accessible language ↔\nTo make their publications correctly understood by the public\nThere could be misunderstanding of the findings presented by the researcher because of\ntechnical terms.\n↔ correctly understood by the public\n选项 To make their publications correctly understood by the public 是 P11 整段问\n题（误解）与解决（翻译成通俗语言）的目的合成，与预判完全吻合。\nD) 与原文相反\nTo render their findings acceptable by prominent journals 说\"让顶级期刊接受研\n究\"——这是 P4 中谈到的另一动机（评 tenure 时投顶级期刊），且 P1 明确批判\"留在\n期刊里就发挥不了作用\"；聘请写手的目的恰恰是\"走出期刊面向公众\"，方向相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What do we learn about spiders in the UK since the middle ages?", "options": ["A) They have been generally misconceived.", "B) They have adversely impacted crop growth.", "C) They have been a constant reminder of bad luck.", "D) They have made their presence felt when spinning webs."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn about spiders in the UK since the middle ages? 用 What do we\nlearn about 引出，问的是关于某对象的客观事实，没有 infer/imply/suggest 等推断信号\n【拆句】题干主句是 What do we learn about spiders in the UK since the middle ages，主干是\nWhat do we learn about X，X 是 spiders in the UK，后面 since the middle ages 是表\n时间起点的介词短语，整体平铺直叙、无陷阱。\n【定位】But they are a constant source of human anxieties—with a cultural association with witches\nand wickedness dating back to the middle ages.\n但它们一直是人类焦虑的来源——与女巫和邪恶的文化关联可追溯至中世纪。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\na cultural association with witches and wickedness ↔ generally misconceived\nits prevalence appears unrelated to any rational assessment of risk ↔ misconceived\n预判落在蜘蛛被文化误解上，A They have been generally misconceived 用\nmisconceived 概括了原文女巫、邪恶等不实联想，与预判完全对应，是定位句的高度抽\n象同义替换。\nB) 与原文相反\nB 选项 They have adversely impacted crop growth 称蜘蛛对农作物生长有不利影\n响，但原文 Farmland species perform valuable ecosystem services, by preying on\ninsects that are our competitors for crops 明确说农田蜘蛛吃掉与人类争粮的昆虫，\n是有利的；B 把有利说成有害，与原文相反。\nC) 偷换概念\nC 选项 They have been a constant reminder of bad luck 说蜘蛛是坏运气的提醒，但\n原文说的是与女巫、邪恶（witches and wickedness）的文化关联，\nwitches/wickedness 是邪恶/巫术，不等于坏运气（bad luck）；二者是不同的文化概\n念，C 偷换了概念。\nD) 答非所问\nD 选项 They have made their presence felt when spinning webs 取自首段 Spiders\nmake their presence felt in late August and through early autumn，讲的是晚夏初\n秋的自然现象，与题干 since the middle ages 的时间范围毫无关系；属答非所问。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What have spiders been associated with in the UK for centuries?", "options": ["A) Harm.", "B) Evil.", "C) Suffering.", "D) Aggression."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What have spiders been associated with in the UK for centuries? 用 What 引出过去完\n成时的事实问句，问的是被关联的对象，没有任何推断信号\n【拆句】题干主干是 What have spiders been associated with，spiders 为主语，谓语为现在完成\n时被动 have been associated with，介词 with 后的宾语正是要回答的内容；in the UK 是\n地点状语，for centuries 是时间状语，结构清晰。\n【定位】But they are a constant source of human anxieties—with a cultural association with witches\nand wickedness dating back to the middle ages.\n但它们一直是人类焦虑的来源——与女巫和邪恶的文化关联可追溯至中世纪。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nA Harm 意为有形的伤害，对应原文应是 spiders 对人造成的实质损害；但原文\nSpiders in the UK are almost all harmless 明确说英国蜘蛛几乎都无害——蜘蛛被关\n联的是文化上的邪恶，而不是实际伤害；A 偷换概念。\nB) ✓ 正确\nwickedness ↔ Evil\nwitches ↔ Evil\n由预判 与巫术与邪恶相关联，选项 B Evil 把 witches 与 wickedness 两个并列宾语用一\n个上位词 Evil 同时归纳，是定位句的精准同义替换。\nC) 张冠李戴\nC Suffering 指痛苦/折磨，原文 Fear of spiders is common and has serious impacts\non the lives of sufferers 出现 sufferers 一词——但 sufferers 描述的是怕蜘蛛的人所\n承受的影响，而题干问蜘蛛被关联的对象，主体错位；C 张冠李戴。\nD) 无中生有\nD Aggression 指攻击性，原文从未把蜘蛛与攻击性联系起来——焦虑、恐惧、邪恶都\n不等于攻击；这是文中没有的概念，属无中生有。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What partly accounts for the reduction in spiders' food supply?", "options": ["A) The long tradition of collecting insects.", "B) Fast reproduction of their competitors.", "C) Chemicals used for killing insects.", "D) The extinction of a lot of wildlife"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What partly accounts for the reduction in spiders' food supply? 用 What accounts\nfor 直接问原因，是典型的因果型细节题，不是推断\n【拆句】题干主干是 What partly accounts for X，partly 修饰 accounts for，X 为名词短语 the\nreduction in spiders' food supply，其中 spiders' 是所有格修饰 food supply；句子结构\n简单，重点是 partly 这一限定词。\n【定位】A recent landmark study identified a 75% fall in insect populations between 1989 and\n2016, with pesticide use thought to be to blame along with the destruction of wild areas\nfor development.\n近期一项标志性研究发现，1989 到 2016 年间昆虫种群下降了 75%，元凶是杀虫剂使用以\n及为开发而破坏野生区域。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\nA The long tradition of collecting insects 取自 British butterflies are the most\nstudied group of insects in the world, due to the long tradition of collecting and\nobserving them，那里讲的是蝴蝶被研究多的原因，与蜘蛛食物减少没有任何因果关\n系；A 答非所问。\nB) 无中生有\nB Fast reproduction of their competitors 称蜘蛛竞争者快速繁殖导致食物减少；但原\n文 preying on insects that are our competitors for crops 说的是昆虫是人类的庄稼\n竞争者，主体是人类的庄稼竞争者，并非蜘蛛的竞争者；且原文从未提及 fast\nreproduction；属无中生有。\nC) ✓ 正确\npesticide use ↔ Chemicals used for killing insects\nthought to be to blame ↔ Chemicals used for killing insects\n由预判 杀虫剂，C Chemicals used for killing insects 是 pesticide 的标准释义，是定\n位句最直接的同义替换，无任何外加信息。\nD) 过度推断\nD The extinction of a lot of wildlife 称大量野生动物灭绝；原文说的是 the\ndestruction of wild areas for development——破坏野生区（栖息地），不是野生动\n物灭绝；二者范围与程度都不同，D 过度推断。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What does the passage say is conservationists' biggest worry?", "options": ["A) A variety of spiders are threatened due to pollution of marshes and wasteland.", "B) Certain species of spiders are endangered due to loss of their natural homes.", "C) An increasing number of spiders are being killed by deadly pesticides.", "D) More and more spider species are found losing their ability to migrate."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What does the passage say is conservationists' biggest worry? 用 What does the\npassage say 直接问文中陈述的事实/观点，是细节题\n【拆句】题干是嵌套疑问句：主干 What does the passage say is X，间接引语 the passage say\n(that) X is conservationists’ biggest worry 被改写成 What...is...的疑问形式；要回答的是\nWhat 所代的 X，即保护者最担心的事，结构稍复杂但无陷阱。\n【定位】But conservationists are most concerned about those varieties that are threatened due to\nhabitat loss and fragmentation, which makes it impossible for them to migrate.\n但保护者最担忧的是那些因栖息地丧失与碎片化而受威胁的物种，这种碎片化使得它们无法\n迁徙。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nA A variety of spiders are threatened due to pollution of marshes and wasteland\n称蜘蛛因沼泽与荒地受污染而受威胁；但原文 the majority thrive in marshes and\nwasteland 说蜘蛛多在沼泽与荒地繁衍，从未提到污染；A 用了原文出现的 marshes\nand wasteland 字眼，但污染（pollution）是无中生有。\nB) ✓ 正确\nthose varieties ↔ Certain species of spiders\nare threatened ↔ are endangered\nhabitat loss and fragmentation ↔ loss of their natural homes\n由预判 栖息地丧失威胁某些物种，B Certain species of spiders are endangered due\nto loss of their natural homes 与原文 those varieties、are threatened、habitat\nloss 三处词一一对应，是定位句的高度同义替换。\nC) 答非所问\nC An increasing number of spiders are being killed by deadly pesticides 称越来越\n多蜘蛛被杀虫剂直接杀死；但原文 pesticide use thought to be to blame 说杀虫剂杀\n的是昆虫（飞虫）、进而影响蜘蛛食物，并非杀虫剂直接杀蜘蛛；C 答非所问，且与题\n干 biggest worry 不符。\nD) 过度推断\nD More and more spider species are found losing their ability to migrate 称越来\n越多物种正失去迁徙能力；原文 habitat loss and fragmentation, which makes it\nimpossible for them to migrate 说的是栖息地丧失与碎片化导致无法迁徙——无法迁\n徙是栖息地丧失的结果，而题干问的是最担忧的原因/对象；D 把结果当作主担忧，且加\n入了原文没有的 more and more（递增趋势），属过度推断。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What wish does the author express close to the end of the passage?", "options": ["A) More people would recognise spiders' unique status in the ecosystem.", "B) People would show greater enthusiasm for spiders than for butterflies.", "C) There would be sufficient corridors for spiders to move along.", "D) There would be more people appreciating spiders' splendour."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What wish does the author express close to the end of the passage? 直接问作者在末\n尾表达的具体愿望，是局部细节题，不是整篇主旨/态度题\n【拆句】题干主干 What wish does the author express，倒装疑问句，主语 the author、谓语\nexpress、宾语 what wish；close to the end of the passage 是位置状语，限定答题范围\n在末段附近；句子无生词、无难句。\n【定位】But as they reveal themselves in all their splendour this autumn, it would be a good thing if\nmore animal lovers recognised the ways in which spiders are simply terrific.\n但当它们在这个秋天展现出全部精彩时，如果有更多动物爱好者能认识到蜘蛛之妙，那真是\n一件好事。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nA More people would recognise spiders' unique status in the ecosystem 称作者希\n望更多人认识到蜘蛛在生态系统中的独特地位；但原文 in spite of their unique\nstatus as nature's spinners 说蜘蛛的独特地位是 纺织者，而非 生态系统中的地位；\n且 unique status 出现在 in spite of 让步状语里，不是作者愿望句的内容；A 偷换了\n概念。\nB) 与原文相反\nB People would show greater enthusiasm for spiders than for butterflies 称作者希\n望人们对蜘蛛比对蝴蝶更热情；但原文 It seems unlikely that spiders will ever attract\nthe same level of human enthusiasm as bees, birds or butterflies 是作者承认蜘蛛\n不太可能 像蜜蜂/鸟/蝴蝶那样受欢迎，这是事实判断而非作者愿望——B 把作者认为不\n可能的对照句反向当成愿望，与原文相反。\nC) 答非所问\nC There would be sufficient corridors for spiders to move along 称作者希望有足够\n的廊道供蜘蛛通行；但 wildlife corridors 出现在 spiders will be enabled to migrate\nby themselves, adapting to climate change by moving along wildlife corridors，\n属于上一段保护政策的内容；题干已限定 close to the end of the passage，C 位置不\n对、答非所问。\nD) ✓ 正确\nmore animal lovers ↔ more people\nrecognised the ways in which spiders are simply terrific ↔ appreciating spiders' splendour\n由预判 更多人能欣赏到蜘蛛的精彩，D There would be more people appreciating\nspiders' splendour 用 more people 概括 animal lovers，用 appreciating spiders’\nsplendour 概括 recognised the ways in which spiders are simply terrific——一一\n对应，是定位句的精准同义替换。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}], "listeningSharedWith": "2023-12-1"});
